1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,970
Checking assumptions and asking questions is

2
00:00:02,970 --> 00:00:06,165
a great follow on after solution sketches had been presented.

3
00:00:06,165 --> 00:00:10,860
It's super easy to get caught in a specific way of thinking or train of thought.

4
00:00:10,860 --> 00:00:13,005
In order to combat this,

5
00:00:13,005 --> 00:00:15,780
we'll identify and question assumptions.

6
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:20,480
Go through each sketch and take a few moments to list out assumptions as a group,

7
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:24,000
then create questions using those assumptions

8
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,395
which will force you to consider possibilities you might not have otherwise considered.

9
00:00:28,395 --> 00:00:32,265
Are things actually necessary or are we just accustomed to them?

10
00:00:32,265 --> 00:00:35,820
Here are some examples of assumptions and questions.

11
00:00:35,820 --> 00:00:37,670
Let's look at a telephone.

12
00:00:37,670 --> 00:00:39,245
About a decade ago,

13
00:00:39,245 --> 00:00:42,830
an assumption would have been that a telephone has to have buttons.

14
00:00:42,830 --> 00:00:45,380
But if we question that assumption,

15
00:00:45,380 --> 00:00:47,975
we would ask, does a phone really need buttons?

16
00:00:47,975 --> 00:00:50,410
Or what if there were no buttons on a phone?

17
00:00:50,410 --> 00:00:52,880
It's this type of thinking that got us to

18
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:56,225
touchscreens where buttons are actually rendered through software.

19
00:00:56,225 --> 00:00:58,775
Another example would be shoes.

20
00:00:58,775 --> 00:01:01,835
The assumption is that shoes have laces,

21
00:01:01,835 --> 00:01:04,030
but if we question that,

22
00:01:04,030 --> 00:01:06,630
what if shoes didn't have laces?

23
00:01:06,630 --> 00:01:10,400
If we look at software in general a couple of decades ago,

24
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:14,420
a common assumption is that you have to have a save button in order

25
00:01:14,420 --> 00:01:18,910
to save your work in progress for pretty much every type of application.

26
00:01:18,910 --> 00:01:21,119
But if we question that assumption,

27
00:01:21,119 --> 00:01:23,190
what if there was no save button?

28
00:01:23,190 --> 00:01:26,120
You'll have noticed that a number of software products over

29
00:01:26,120 --> 00:01:30,415
the years no longer require users to explicitly hit a save button.

30
00:01:30,415 --> 00:01:34,445
Instead, they're constantly saving the progress that a user makes.

31
00:01:34,445 --> 00:01:38,675
We just walked through a few examples of assumptions and how you can question them.

32
00:01:38,675 --> 00:01:42,740
Sometimes, questioning an assumption might feel a little bit crazy,

33
00:01:42,740 --> 00:01:45,500
but oftentimes, it can lead to good innovation.

34
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:49,640
After you've gone through assumptions and questions for each solution sketch,

35
00:01:49,640 --> 00:01:53,120
you'll want to identify common assumptions and questions.

36
00:01:53,120 --> 00:01:54,920
You should make a special note of

37
00:01:54,920 --> 00:01:57,170
those overlapping questions to make

38
00:01:57,170 --> 00:02:00,810
sure that you answer them during the validation phase.

